sequenceDiagram
  %% schedule.enable of a retained host Node timer on the Gateway engine: the timer path of schedule/enable.mmd (workflow operation-schedule-control) as one executor primitive. A `production_control` plan (workflow production-schedule-control: the timer inside the selected live release) stays on Swamp. ADR 0267 and ADR 0271; ScheduleEnableEngineFlow, primitive `schedule.ensure_timer`. Runtime proof on a nested fleet is pending.
  %% A native cron Schedule has no Node timer: it keeps the synchronous Swamp trigger set and its succeeded Operation, with no executor order.
  %% status: built
  %% command: schedule:enable
  %% kind: schedule.enable
  autonumber
  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray
  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue
  %% lane EN icon=workflow pill=engine tone=purple
  %% lane N icon=server pill=executor tone=amber
  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools
  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API
  participant EN as Engine<br/>Gateway and NATS
  participant N as Target Node<br/>executor
  CLI->>GW: schedule:enable 7f3c…<br/>Schedule UUID
  Note over GW: Validate the Schedule<br/>Schedule 7f3c… · Instance 42
  %%> Same validation as schedule/enable.mmd
  alt native cron Schedule
    Note over GW: Set the Swamp trigger<br/>no executor order
    %%> As schedule/enable.mmd (NativeSchedules::enable): the Gateway sets the workflow trigger; after Swamp confirms it records a succeeded `schedule.enable` Operation with its result and answers with its ID; a refusal keeps the prior state and records none
    %%> The engine admits nothing here; no run, no Node order
  else production_control plan
    Note over GW: Refuse the engine<br/>EngineUnsupported
    %%> A timer inside a production release container (`production_control`): verifying the selected live release, enabling the timer inside it and updating its release record stay on production-schedule-control; the whole Operation runs there
  else retained host Node timer
    alt the engine admits the whole Operation
      %%> Admission, one decision per Operation (EngineKinds::routes):
      %%> - `schedule.enable` is in `ORBIT_ENGINE_KINDS`
      %%> - every Node a step touches reports its action in `$KV.NODES`: the timer action of the Schedule's Node
      %%> - the flow accepts the plan shape; a part it does not cover throws `EngineUnsupported` before any order
      %%> - eligible plan: a host timer on the Schedule's Node, no `production_control` and no `container`
      Note over GW: Bind steps, take the lock<br/>commit, publish the start
      %%> - the step rows and the Schedule's own Operation lock (`schedule:<uuid>`; the owner's other Schedules stay free) commit in one transaction before the start event; an Operation never moves between engines after this
      %%> - binding refuses `operation.target_busy` while another open Operation acts on this Schedule; while the lock is held, any admission for this Schedule, a create's reservation included, refuses `operation.target_busy` before it changes the row
      GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>task ID
      EN->>N: Enable and start the timer<br/>owned unit only
      %%> Desired state: the Schedule's own timer is enabled and active; an already-running timer only gets its enablement checked
      %%> The unit name comes from the admitted Schedule; any other unit refuses with `schedule.input_invalid`
      N-->>EN: Observed timer<br/>enabled · active · next elapse
      %%> Verification read-back in the same primitive, as Swamp's `Check the timer`: LoadState=loaded, UnitFileState=enabled, ActiveState=active, and NextElapseUSecRealtime answered (empty when the calendar has none left); a property systemd leaves out fails the step
      opt the receipt is lost
        EN->>N: Resend the same step<br/>fresh nonce · same step ID
        %%> When:
        %%> - the step passes its resend deadline with no receipt; two sends, one execution
        %%> - the Operation keeps its lock, so Schedule 7f3c… stays fenced; no other Operation and no Swamp run acts on it meanwhile
        %%> On the Node:
        %%> - the journal holds `done` for this Operation step, action and target: the executor answers the stored result with `repeat: true` and does not act again
        %%> - an order still `open` replays under the target lock first; an order that expired before the lock did nothing (`executor.order_expired`), and the step is sent again
        N-->>EN: Stored result<br/>repeat: true
      end
      alt every step verified
        EN->>GW: Record Schedule enabled<br/>desired state: enabled
        %%> - the kind's own projection records the result, the same rows a Swamp run records
      else a step fails
        EN--xGW: Failed step<br/>error_code · redacted detail
        %%> - a deterministic refusal is a terminal receipt too; the journal keeps it and a resend repeats it
        %%> - the step keeps a code for its phase (`schedule.input_invalid`, `schedule.timer_missing`, `schedule.apply_failed` for the change, `schedule.read_failed` for the read-back read, `schedule.state_mismatch` for a read of another state); the Operation records what Swamp records: `operation.native_failed`, failed step `timer`, `check_timer` or `Check the timer`; a cancel records no error code, the step code in the failure's detail
        Note over GW: Keep the prior state<br/>desired state unchanged
        %%> - retry: the same command; enabling an enabled timer changes nothing
      end
    else the engine does not admit it
      Note over GW: Start the Swamp run<br/>operation-schedule-control
      %%> - the kind is not enabled, a Node lacks the action, `$KV.NODES` is unreadable, or the flow throws `EngineUnsupported`: the whole Operation runs operation-schedule-control (schedule/enable.mmd)
      %%> - no step of it runs on the engine; an Operation never splits across engines
    end
  end
